[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Template Syntax & Directives in Svelte"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte's template syntax is designed to be familiar to anyone who knows HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Unlike traditional frameworks that introduce heavy runtime abstractions, Svelte compiles templates directly into highly optimized imperative DOM operations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Dynamic Text & Attributes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Injecting dynamic data into your markup is achieved using curly braces {}. This syntax applies to both text content and HTML attributes."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  let name = 'World';
  let src = 'https://svelte.dev/svelte-logo.svg';
</script>

<h1>Hello {name}!</h1>
<img {src} alt="Svelte Logo" />"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Notice how the image source uses shorthand attribute syntax ({src}). If your attribute name matches the variable name, you can omit the value assignment."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Conditional Rendering"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Svelte handles conditional logic natively inside the template using block syntax. You can use {#if}, {:else if}, and {:else} blocks."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  let user = { loggedIn: false };
  function toggleLogin() {
    user.loggedIn = !user.loggedIn;
  }
</script>

{#if user.loggedIn}
  <button on:click={toggleLogin}>Log out</button>
{:else}
  <button on:click={toggleLogin}>Log in</button>
{/if}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"List Rendering with keyed each"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To render arrays of data, use the {#each} block. Providing a unique key (e.g., (item.id)) ensures that Svelte efficiently updates the DOM when items are reordered or removed."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  let cats = [
    { id: 'Jup', name: 'Jupiter' },
    { id: 'Sig', name: 'Sigmund' },
    { id: 'Pel', name: 'Peluche' }
  ];
</script>

<ul>
  {#each cats as cat, i (cat.id)}
    <li>{i + 1}: {cat.name}</li>
  {/each}
</ul>"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Event Directives"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Event listeners are attached to DOM elements using the on:directive. Modifiers can be chained directly to the event name to handle common patterns like preventDefault or once."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  let count = 0;
  function handleClick() {
    count += 1;
  }
</script>

<button on:click|once={handleClick}>
  Click me once (Clicked {count} times)
</button>"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Binding Directives"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Data flow in Svelte is normally one-way (from parent to child, or state to view). However, user inputs require two-way data binding. The bind: directive simplifies synchronizing form elements with component state."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  let message = 'Hello Svelte!';
</script>

<input bind:value={message} />
<p>{message}</p>"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use curly braces {} for expressions, text interpolation, and attribute values."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Control flow blocks like {#if}, {#each}, and {#await} require a matching closing tag {/if}, {/each}, or {/await}."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Directives use a colon (on:click, bind:value, class:active) to apply special Svelte behaviors."}]
